\section{Choose the State and the Widths}

An instruction says how to transform the current machine state using operands recovered from one instruction word. Architectural state is the part a program can observe through the ISA: register contents, memory, the program counter, and any flags or other special registers defined by the architecture. A pipeline latch holding a copy of an operand is not another architectural register. Sections~3.2--3.3 of the February 2024 VADL preprint, arXiv:2402.09087v1, introduce types and resources for this distinction \cite{himmelbauer2024vadl}.

The excerpt below is normalized VADL from Listing~2. It assumes the surrounding ISA block; the memory ordering annotation in the listing is not shown here.

\begin{verbatim}
using Byte = Bits<8>
using Regs = Bits<Size>
using Addr = Regs
using Index = Bits<5>

[littleEndian]
memory MEM : Addr -> Byte
[current]
program counter PC : Addr
[X(0) = 0]
register file X : Index -> Regs
\end{verbatim}

Here \texttt{Size} is 32. The register index has five bits, independently of the width of a register value. The memory mapping is byte-addressed; the annotation governs how a multi-byte quantity is laid out, not the significance of the bits in a register. The hardwired zero cell is a resource invariant, not a requirement that programmers refrain from using that index. Distinguish \texttt{Bool}, a condition type, from \texttt{Bits<N>}, which stores a vector of fixed width. The arithmetic subtypes \texttt{SInt<N>} and \texttt{UInt<N>} give operations a signed or unsigned interpretation. A bit pattern can compare differently under those two interpretations. A cast using \texttt{as} can also extend or truncate at a width boundary, so moving a cast across an operation is not an innocuous refactoring.

\section{Bind Values Before Updating Resources}

Listing~2 gives the following instruction body; \texttt{Btype} binds the register fields and gives the instruction a derived displacement \texttt{immS}.

\begin{verbatim}
instruction BEQ : Btype = {
  let cond = X(rs1) = X(rs2) in
  if cond then
    PC := PC + immS
}
\end{verbatim}

The equality is a value computation; \texttt{:=} updates a resource. Section~3.3 describes the instruction language as single assignment and requires reads of a register or memory location to precede writes to that location. A \texttt{let} binding names an already computed value, not a mutable temporary. With those rules, reading the old content of a register and writing its new content is legal. Writing it and then reading it back as an intermediate computation should instead be expressed by retaining the computed value. This matters even if different field names identify the resources: a source and a destination field may both name the same physical register.

The mandatory program counter has an interpretation too. In this example \texttt{[current]} exposes the address of the instruction being executed. Section~3.3 also discusses \texttt{[next]} and \texttt{[next next]} for other architectural conventions. In the absence of an explicit update, the PC advances by the instruction's size. Do not mistake the absence of an \texttt{else} clause for a branch that keeps the program counter unchanged.

