\section{Follow Both Paths from One Decoded Word}

This is a teaching trace of the rule, not VADL source. The already decoded branch uses distinct source indices; its displacement is in bytes.

\begin{verbatim}
input: PC = 0x100, width = 4, rs1 = 5, rs2 = 6, immS = 12

X(5) = 7, X(6) = 7  -> cond = true
  PC' = 0x100 + 12 = 0x10c   # explicit architectural update

X(5) = 7, X(6) = 8  -> cond = false
  PC' = 0x100 + 4  = 0x104   # implicit sequential successor
\end{verbatim}

As \Cref{fig:visual-324} shows, the tests share the word and differ only in one old register value. In both cases the register file and memory are unchanged. No extra four bytes should be added on the taken path. Conversely, testing a displacement of four alone would fail to distinguish an always-falling-through implementation from the true branch behavior.

