\section{Check What State Transformations Preserve}

VIR makes resource access explicit. The paper's ADD listing in Section~4.3 shows register reads before the write and represents the sequential PC advance in the instruction process. An optimizer can simplify pure computations without removing architecturally visible writes. It also needs to distinguish a dead arithmetic result from an exceptional effect; Section~3.3's MIPS example uses \texttt{raise} explicitly rather than burying exceptions in ordinary assignments.

For example, an implementation may common a repeated pure comparison only when it preserves the values sampled before resource updates:

\begin{verbatim}
# VIR-like pseudocode, before a write to X
same = read(X, rs1) == read(X, rs2)
if same: write(PC, read(PC) + immS)

# Legal optimization: use `same` at each later use.
# Illegal rewrite: reread X after an intervening write and call it `same`.
\end{verbatim}

The pseudocode is deliberately not VADL syntax: it makes the ordering obligation visible to an optimizer or generator author. The generated ISS, decoder tests, and hardware implementation may use different internal representations, but each must retain the old-state comparison and the implicit sequential PC rule.

If a new operation changes an unsigned condition to a signed one, keep the bytes fixed in the regression test and compare operands with the high bit set. A test over small nonnegative values cannot reveal that edit. If the change alters the PC convention, check both control-flow targets and call return addresses under the regenerated tools. Static checking of types and read/write discipline supports those tests; it does not replace them. Section~5.1 evaluates expressiveness across example ISAs, not semantic equivalence of any arbitrary description to a reference machine.
